  {
    "conversation": "Doctor: Hi Ms. Kim, I'm Dr. Carter. How are you doing today?\nPatient: Hello, Dr. Carter. I'm doing okay, but I'm here because of this lump on my wrist.\nDoctor: Alright. Can you tell me more about this lump and how long you've had it?\nPatient: Sure. I've noticed this round lump on the back of my right wrist for about a month now. It causes mild to moderate discomfort, and sometimes it feels numb.\nDoctor: I see. How often do you experience this discomfort and numbness?\nPatient: It's intermittent, but it gets worse when I flex my wrist or lift heavy objects. It\u2019s starting to limit my activities a bit.\nDoctor: That sounds inconvenient, especially with your work. What do you do for a living?\nPatient: I work from home as a graphic designer, so I use my hands a lot. I also like to knit and paint in my free time, which sometimes makes the discomfort worse.\nDoctor: I understand. Let\u2019s go over a few more details to get a complete picture. Have you noticed any persistent numbness or tingling beyond the wrist?\nPatient: No, the numbness is only occasional and just in the wrist area.\nDoctor: Any other symptoms or systemic complaints?\nPatient: No, nothing else. Just the lump and the discomfort in the wrist.\nDoctor: Alright. Let's review your medical history. Do you have any past diagnoses or chronic conditions?\nPatient: I have migraines, but nothing chronic other than that.\nDoctor: And for the migraines, are you on any medication?\nPatient: Yes, I take Sumatriptan 50 mg as needed when I have a migraine.\nDoctor: Any allergies we should be aware of?\nPatient: I'm allergic to shellfish.\nDoctor: Got it. How about your family history? Any significant medical conditions in your family?\nPatient: My sister has lupus, but that's about it.\nDoctor: Thank you for sharing that. Now, let\u2019s touch upon your social history. What\u2019s your lifestyle like?\nPatient: I follow a predominantly vegetarian diet, practice yoga three times a week, and occasionally go jogging.\nDoctor: Do you use any tobacco, alcohol, or drugs?\nPatient: No, I don\u2019t use any of those.\nDoctor: Alright, let's proceed with a physical examination. I'll start by inspecting the lump on your wrist.\nPatient: Okay.\nDoctor: (Examining wrist) I see a visible, round lump on the dorsal aspect of your right wrist. It feels smooth, firm, and slightly mobile. Does it hurt when I press here?\nPatient: A bit, yes.\nDoctor: Let's check your range of motion. Can you flex your wrist for me?\nPatient: Sure. (Flexes wrist)\nDoctor: I see you have a full range of motion, but it looks like it\u2019s causing you some discomfort. I\u2019m going to perform a transillumination test now. (Turns on light) The cyst lights up, which is consistent with a ganglion cyst.\nPatient: What does that mean?\nDoctor: A ganglion cyst is a noncancerous lump that often develops along the tendons or joints of your wrists or hands. It's generally harmless but can cause discomfort.\nPatient: Oh, I see.\nDoctor: I\u2019m going to document your vital signs now. Your blood pressure is 120/80 mmHg, heart rate is 72 bpm, respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute, and your temperature is 98.6\u00b0F. Everything looks normal there.\nPatient: That\u2019s reassuring.\nDoctor: Based on your symptoms and the examination, it appears you have a ganglion cyst on your wrist. We can proceed with aspiration to relieve the discomfort. This involves using a needle to drain the fluid from the cyst. Does that sound okay to you?\nPatient: Yes, that sounds good. I just want the discomfort to go away.\nDoctor: Great. After the aspiration, I\u2019ll provide you with a wrist brace to support your wrist during activities that exacerbate your symptoms. You can also take Acetaminophen 500 mg every six hours as needed for pain.\nPatient: Okay, thank you.\nDoctor: I\u2019ll perform the aspiration now. (Performs aspiration) All done. How do you feel?\nPatient: It feels a bit better already.\nDoctor: That\u2019s good to hear. I want you to follow up in two weeks so we can monitor for any recurrence of the cyst. I\u2019ll also refer you to an orthopedic specialist, Dr. John Smith, in case the cyst comes back and we need to consider surgical removal.\nPatient: Alright, I\u2019ll make sure to follow up.\nDoctor: I also want to discuss the nature of ganglion cysts with you. They can sometimes recur, so it\u2019s important to monitor for any signs that would necessitate earlier follow-up or intervention.\nPatient: What should I look out for?\nDoctor: Watch for any increase in size, persistent pain, or changes in the lump's characteristics. Avoid activities that might exacerbate the symptoms, and use the wrist brace as needed.\nPatient: Understood.\nDoctor: Do you have any concerns about accessing the specialist?\nPatient: I was a bit worried about that, but I\u2019m glad you\u2019re arranging the referrals and follow-up care.\nDoctor: Don't worry, we\u2019ll make sure you get the care you need. You\u2019ve been very cooperative and eager to follow the medical advice, which is great.\nPatient: Thank you, Dr. Carter. I really appreciate it.\nDoctor: You\u2019re welcome, Ms. Kim. If you have any questions or if the discomfort persists, please don\u2019t hesitate to contact us. See you in two weeks.\nPatient: Will do. Thank you again.",
    "subjective": "The patient reports intermittent mild to moderate wrist discomfort, particularly when flexing the wrist or lifting heavy objects, which is starting to limit her activities.",
    "objective": "The patient has a round lump on the back of her right wrist that she has noticed for about a month. She describes it as causing mild to moderate discomfort and numbness in the affected area.",
    "assessment": "Based on the patient's symptoms and physical examination, it appears that she may be experiencing carpal tunnel syndrome or another form of repetitive strain injury affecting her wrists.",
    "plan": "Further evaluation is recommended to determine the underlying cause of the patient's symptoms. A thorough medical history and physical examination will be performed to rule out other potential causes. The patient should also be advised on ways to manage her symptoms, such as taking regular breaks when using her hands, avoiding heavy lifting, and considering ergonomic modifications in her workspace. Note: This SOAP note is a general template and may need to be modified based on the specific needs of the patient and the clinical conversation."
  },
